Subject: [PATCH] venus: hide host access to a small BAR heap

Without resizable BAR, the host's NVIDIA driver offers host-visible VRAM
as a heap of its own, 246 MiB on an RTX 5090. On the host that heap is
the card's BAR1 aperture (256 MiB), which the host desktop, every other
process and every VM on the card share. A Venus guest's host-visible blob
costs twice its size there: vkAllocateMemory in the render server maps it
into BAR1, and the dma-buf mmap that virglrenderer makes in QEMU for the
guest's blob mapping maps it again. The driver admits an allocation while
the heap has room for one copy, so with a few VMs on a card the second
mapping fails: mmap gets ENOMEM and the host kernel logs
"NVRM: dmaAllocMapping_GM107: can't alloc VA space for mapping".

Memory types that are device-local and host-visible on a heap less than
half the size of the largest device-local heap now report as
device-local only, so applications, DXVK, vkd3d-proton and Zink put
host-visible allocations in system memory. Resizable-BAR heaps and
integrated GPUs keep their host-visible VRAM type.

Evidence: docs/nvva.md, docs/evidence/nvva/.
Origin: vmlab-win3d nvva worker.
---
--- a/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_physical_device.c	2026-10-19 04:24:49.764066631 +1000
+++ b/src/virtio/vulkan/vn_physical_device.c	2026-10-19 04:25:44.490924877 +1000
@@ -974,6 +974,40 @@
    return VK_SUCCESS;
 }
 
+/* Without resizable BAR, a discrete GPU exposes host-visible VRAM through a
+ * small separate heap (246 MiB on an RTX 5090). On the host that window is
+ * the card's BAR1, which every process and every VM on the card shares, and
+ * a guest blob of it costs twice its size there: the allocation and the
+ * dma-buf mapping that QEMU makes of it. When BAR1 runs out, the host driver
+ * still admits the allocation but the mapping fails ("can't alloc VA space
+ * for mapping" in the host kernel log) and the guest's ring stalls. Report
+ * such types as device-local only, so host-visible allocations land in
+ * system memory. A BAR heap at least half the size of the largest
+ * device-local heap (resizable BAR, or an integrated GPU) is left alone.
+ */
+static void
+vn_physical_device_hide_small_bar_memory(
+   VkPhysicalDeviceMemoryProperties *props)
+{
+   VkDeviceSize vram = 0;
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < props->memoryHeapCount; i++) {
+      if (props->memoryHeaps[i].flags & VK_MEMORY_HEAP_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT)
+         vram = MAX2(vram, props->memoryHeaps[i].size);
+   }
+
+   const VkMemoryPropertyFlags bar = VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_DEVICE_LOCAL_BIT |
+                                     VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT;
+   for (uint32_t i = 0; i < props->memoryTypeCount; i++) {
+      VkMemoryType *type = &props->memoryTypes[i];
+      if ((type->propertyFlags & bar) != bar ||
+          props->memoryHeaps[type->heapIndex].size * 2 > vram)
+         continue;
+      type->propertyFlags &= ~(VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_VISIBLE_BIT |
+                               VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_COHERENT_BIT |
+                               VK_MEMORY_PROPERTY_HOST_CACHED_BIT);
+   }
+}
+
 static void
 vn_physical_device_init_memory_properties(
    struct vn_physical_device *physical_dev)
@@ -988,6 +1022,9 @@
 
    physical_dev->memory_properties = props2.memoryProperties;
 
+   vn_physical_device_hide_small_bar_memory(
+      &physical_dev->memory_properties);
+
    /* Kernel makes every mapping coherent. If a memory type is truly
     * incoherent, it's better to remove the host-visible flag than silently
     * making it coherent. However, for app compatibility purpose, when
